# A `;` ends the `if` statement, so an `else` after it starts a statement of
# its own, which no statement can. Subtle: C writes `if (c) a; else b;`, so
# this is a likely habit, and it gets the hint on the conditional's form.
#= {
#=     rejects: ['found `else`', 'conditional is written `if a: b else: c`'],
#=     inputs: {c: true},
#=     known_issue: 'FROST-159',
#= }
if c: 1; else: 2
